 is our position at  conference we agreed a wide-ranging  policy that stated that we wanted to  have a general election in the event  that Theresa Mays deal fell and that was  to avoid an old deal impasse but also to  ensure that we could put forward our  deal that would provide a consensus  across Parliament in the event that all  of that fails we wanted to keep all  options on the table because it's  critical to avoid a no deal situation  and okay so just trying to kind of  unpick you onto there it sounds like you  would be campaigning for brexit but a  different kind of deal negotiated now  just trying to kind of really get some  clarity on what labor wants to do last  week on the show Barry Gardner suggested  that actually what would happen is that  labor would have an election they then  renegotiate a deal and then put that  negotiated deal back to the British  people in a referendum is that party  policy that's not official party policy  at this stage I mean the policy that we  agreed at conference was to call a  general election try to secure the best  deal that we possibly can and in the  event that that's not possible or there  are any other issues we had all options  on the table and the specifics of the  policy that would go into our manifesto  you know we're a democratic party that  would be discussed at the appropriate  time when we were putting together the  manifesto but you know to be as clear as  I possibly can we've always said that we  respected the result of the referendum  we've always said that we wanted to  secure a good deal for Britain but in  the event that that seems impossible or  a No Deal situation seems to be hurtling  towards us we've been very clear within  the party that all options will remain  on the table but our priority has always  been making sure that we get that deal  okay and now this week we saw some  pretty horrific images really over MPs  being surrounded by protesters being  labeled Nazis they're being shouted at  and a Seabury the concert to MPs given  an interview in the last week where she  says that she won't even stand close to  the edge of the platform on the London  Underground cause she's worried about  people pushing her into the path of the  Train it's a frightening thing and then  paint a minute well it means certainly  in light of what happened to my  colleague Joe Cox a few years ago I  think it's brought home the reality of  the risks that are involved so members  of political parties but also all the  leading public figures and it's you know  it's not acceptable to be living under  that's the better fear but equally you  expect to be held to account as a  politician you expect your constituents  to tell you what they feel you expect to  be you know held to account for your own  actions as well but not in a way that  makes you feel intimidated or frightened  okay rebecca lynn bailey thank you very  much for being on the program today
 

